<details class="collapsible-section research-overview" open>
  <summary><h2>research overview</h2></summary>
  <div class="section-body">
    <p>
      <span class="overview-topic">Full-stack optimization.</span>
      My work has centered around full-stack optimization of LLM inference workloads. My work has been driven by understanding and optimizing
      bottlenecks across hardware, systems, and algorithms. This work has been guided by our characterization of LLM workloads [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.14017"
        title="Full Stack Optimization of Transformer Inference: A Survey"
        >Full-Stack (ISCA Workshop'23)</a
      >] as well as memory bottlenecks in AI [<a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10412345" title="AI and Memory Wall"
        >AI and Memory Wall (IEEE Micro'24)</a
      >].
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="overview-topic">LLM compression + long context inference.</span>
      In my PhD, the bulk of my work focused on designing memory-efficient algorithms and systems for LLM inference. My work largely centered around
      accelerating LLM decoding by leveraging model compression [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07629"
        title="SqueezeLLM: Dense-and-Sparse Quantization"
        >SqueezeLLM (ICML'24)</a
      >] and speculative decoding [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.10424"
        title="QuantSpec: Self-Speculative Decoding with Hierarchical Quantized KV Cache"
        >QuantSpec (ICML'25)</a
      >]. I also worked extensively on accelerating long context inference workloads through KV cache compression [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.18079"
        title="KVQuant: Towards 10 Million Context Length LLM Inference with KV Cache Quantization"
        >KVQuant (NeurIPS'24)</a
      >], sparse + approximate attention algorithms [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.09688"
        title="Squeezed Attention: Accelerating Long Context Length LLM Inference"
        >Squeezed Attn (ACL'25)</a
      >, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13059" title="Multipole Attention for Efficient Long Context Reasoning">Multipole Attn (NeurIPS'25)</a>],
      and rematerializing the KV cache [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.10395"
        title="XQuant: Breaking the Memory Wall for LLM Inference with KV Cache Rematerialization"
        >XQuant (arXiv'25)</a
      >].
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="overview-topic">Efficient reasoning &amp; agents.</span>
      I have also worked on accelerating LLM applications and alternative LLM architectures. I have designed efficient algorithms for LLM applications
      such as reasoning [<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.13575" title="ETS: Efficient Tree Search for Inference-Time Scaling">ETS (arXiv'25)</a>,
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.05033" title="Arbitrage: Efficient Reasoning via Advantage-Aware Speculation">Arbitrage (arXiv'25)</a>,
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22954" title="Residual Context Diffusion Language Models">RCD (ICML'26)</a>] and agentic workloads [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.00608"
        title="TinyAgent: Function Calling at the Edge"
        >TinyAgent (EMNLP Demo'24)</a
      >,
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07725" title="Squeeze Evolve: Unified Multi-Model Orchestration for Verifier-Free Evolution"
        >Squeeze Evolve (COLM'26)</a
      >].
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="overview-topic">Parallel and looped generation.</span>
      Additionally, to tackle the limited inherent parallelism with LLM generation for small batch sizes [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04146"
        title="Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models"
        >Beyond Next-Token Prediction (arXiv'25)</a
      >], I have worked on alternative LLM architectures which can generate multiple tokens in parallel. I have explored diffusion language models in
      order to draft and iteratively refine multiple tokens in parallel [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12056"
        title="LoSA: Locality Aware Sparse Attention for Block-Wise Diffusion Language Models"
        >LoSA (ICML'26)</a
      >, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.19269" title="CDLM: Consistency Diffusion Language Models for Faster Sampling">CDLM (MLSys'26)</a>], as
      well as self-speculative decoding methods for looped transformers using earlier loops for drafting and later loops for verification [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.12072"
        title="SPEED: Speculative Pipelined Execution for Efficient Decoding"
        >SPEED (Springer'25)</a
      >].
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="overview-topic">LLM inference hardware.</span>
      I have also worked extensively on inference hardware for LLM and Speech Processing Workloads. This includes accelerators and SoCs for
      noise-robust speech and NLP applications [<span class="cite"
        >ASR (<a
          href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9512345"
          title="SM6: A 16nm System-on-Chip for Accurate and Noise-Robust Attention-Based NLP Applications"
          >Hot Chips'21</a
        >,
        <a
          href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9361234"
          title="A 25mm² SoC for IoT Devices with 18ms Noise-Robust Speech-to-Text Latency via Bayesian Speech Denoising and Attention-Based Sequence-to-Sequence DNN Speech Recognition in 16nm FinFET"
          >ISSCC'21</a
        >,
        <a
          href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9876543"
          title="A 16-nm SoC for Noise-Robust Speech and NLP Edge AI Inference with Bayesian Sound Source Separation and Attention-Based DNNs"
          >JSSC'22</a
        >)</span
      >] and for energy-efficient transformer inference [<span class="cite"
        >BERT (<a
          href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.14203"
          title="EdgeBERT: Sentence-Level Energy Optimizations for Latency-Aware Multi-Task NLP Inference"
          >MICRO'21</a
        >,
        <a
          href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10012345"
          title="A 12nm 18.1 TFLOPs/W Sparse Transformer Processor with Entropy-Based Early Exit, Mixed-Precision Predication and Fine-Grained Power Management"
          >ISSCC'23</a
        >)</span
      >]. I have also worked on co-designing quantization and sparsity algorithms with hardware for LLM inference [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.14152"
        title="FGMP: Fine-Grained Mixed-Precision Weight and Activation Quantization for Hardware-Accelerated LLM Inference"
        >FGMP (arXiv'25)</a
      >,
      <span title="Sirius: A Dual-Chiplet System for Multimodal Embodied AI with Heterogeneous RVV Cores, Dense and Sparse Accelerators"
        >Sirius (VLSI'26)</span
      >], and explored the potential of emerging memory technologies like Compute-in-Memory and High-Bandwidth Flash for LLM inference [<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16161"
        title="LLM Inference in a Flash!"
        >LLM-in-a-Flash (ISCA Workshop'26)</a
      >,
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.39131" title="Characterizing High Bandwidth Flash for LLM Serving">Characterizing HBF (MICRO Workshop'26)</a
      >].
    </p>
  </div>
</details>
